This one came from a friend of mine.

In addition to the standard "blindness" effect, there would also be
something called "color-blindness."  It would not show up on the
status line; the only way to know you had it would be by
experimentation or enlightenment/probing/other means of
self-knowledge.  Eating a carrot would cure it just like ordinary
blindness, but it would either have a much longer time out, or not
time-out at all naturally.  Like most natural color-blindness, it
wouldn't usually be the complete inability to see color, but in fact
be the confusion of two colors for each other.  Red/green color
blindness would be most common, with yellow-blue and complete
color-blindness both being much rarer.

All color-blindness does, ultimately, is affect some of the
descriptions given of game objects.  The colors of potions would be
modified, making some appear identical to others (though they still
wouldn't stack together).  This is the biggest problem color-blindness
would cause, and coult result in players mis-identifying things. 
(When the player is color-blind, Nethack would refuse to "obviously"
identify affected potions.  An observant player might infer his
color-blindedness from this.)

Dragons and other color-dependent monsters might be mistaken for each
other.  This wouldn't affect the most dangerous dragon type (black
appears black because of the absence of color), but could result in
green and red dragons being confused.  It would also modify the
descriptions of the current colors accordingly.

The biggest problem with this idea is catching all the descriptions
and modifying them appropriately.  Also, I'm currently unsure what
game events would bring about color-blindness -- maybe a cursed potion
of see invisible?

